>> title: Chả Lụa
>> category: Stews & Braises
>> tags: pork, vietnamese, cold cuts, poached, banh mi
>> counters: Phở & Bánh Mì
>> aka: cha lua, chả lụa, giò lụa, gio lua, Vietnamese pork roll, pork roll, Vietnamese ham, steamed pork sausage, cha lua banh mi, gio
>> pairs-with: banh-mi-khong
>> servings: 12
>> time: 10 hr
>> step.1: freeze firm 45 min
>> step.2: beat cold to a smooth paste 4 min
>> step.3: wrap tight and tie
>> step.4: poach 50 min to 165°F (74°C)
>> step.5: chill 8 hr, then slice

Spread @lean pork loin{2%lb}(900 g; cut in 1-inch cubes) and @pork fatback{4%oz}(115 g; diced) on a tray and ~freeze{45%min}, until the edges are hard and the middle still gives.

Beat @&(~1)cold pork{} with @fish sauce{3%Tbs}(45 mL), @granulated sugar{1%Tbs}(12 g), @baking powder{1%tsp}(4 g), @potato starch{2%Tbs}(20 g), @white pepper{1%tsp} and @ice water{1/4%cup}(60 mL) in a #food processor{} in short bursts ~beat{4%min}, until it is glossy and tacky and pulls away in one piece. This is the line the dish lives or dies on: the paste has to stay below 50°F (10°C) the whole way through. Warmer than that and the fat smears instead of emulsifying, and the roll comes out crumbly where it should be springy. If the bowl feels warm to a knuckle, stop and put the lot back in the freezer for ten minutes.

Wrap @&(~1)pork paste{} in @banana leaves{4%large}(rinsed, then passed over a flame until they go glossy) and again in foil, rolling it into a tight 2-inch log and tying both ends with #kitchen twine{}. Banana leaf is what makes it smell like the cold case; parchment and foil give the same texture and none of the perfume, which is a fair trade if you cannot buy the leaves.

Poach @&(~1)roll{} in @water{4%qt}(3.8 L; at a shiver, never a boil) ~poach{50%min}, until the centre reads 165°F (74°C).

Chill @&(~1)chả lụa{} ~chill{8%hr} before unwrapping. It slices clean and springs back under the knife only once it is cold the whole way through, which is also when it goes into a sandwich.
